 "etapas": [
  {
   "etapa": "explicar",
   "piezas": [
    {
     "tipo": "explicacion",
     "titulo": "Ser or estar",
     "texto": [
      "In Spanish there are two verbs for «to be»:\n\n- **Ser**: identity, profession, origin and regular traits (soy de Madrid, es profesor, Ana es alta).\n- **Estar**: the location of someone or something, and temporary states (estoy en Madrid, está ocupado, estoy cansada).\n\nTo say that you are at home, use **en casa** (without an article): «estoy en casa». Saying «en la casa» refers to a specific physical building.\n\nThe present forms are: **estoy, estás, está, estamos, estáis, están**. The written accent on **está** distinguishes the verb form from «esta» (feminine demonstrative)."
     ],
     "tutora": ""
    },
    {
     "tipo": "gramatica",
     "titulo": "Location and states with estar",
     "texto": [
      "We use **estar** to point out where a person, building or object is, and also to express how we feel:\n\n- The museum **está aquí**; the park **está allí**.\n- Today **estamos cansados**, but tomorrow we will be fine.\n\nRemember: origin takes ser («soy de Lima»), while temporary stay takes estar («estoy en Lima»)."
     ],
